    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-10-7-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/regional-and-community-studies/social-change-in-industrial-regions/",
    "name": "Social Change in Industrial Regions",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/regional-and-community-studies/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Industrial development reshaped communities in Iran through migration, wage employment, housing, public services, and struggles over working conditions. Historical research on Khuzestan's oil towns and Isfahan's manufacturing economy reveals distinct regional paths and unequal distributions of benefits and environmental burdens. [s1][s2][s3][s4][s5]",
    "tags": ["Industrial communities", "Labor history", "Migration", "Khuzestan", "Isfahan", "Environmental history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:51:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:51:00Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Change in Iran's Industrial Regions | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "A historical account of migration, company housing, labor organization, and environmental inequality in Khuzestan and Isfahan."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "industry-and-community",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Industry as a social transformation",
        "content": "Industrialization changes more than what a region produces. In southwestern Iran, oil extraction, refinery construction, docks, and company settlements created new relationships between employers, workers, and surrounding communities. Touraj Atabaki's labor-history study describes recruitment from tribal and village populations and the transition into an industrial workplace with unfamiliar demands and forms of discipline. Wage employment helped create an industrial working class rather than simply transferring an already formed workforce into factories. [s1]\n\nThis history also cautions against assuming that migration meant an immediate or complete break with earlier ways of life. Atabaki presents recruitment itself as a difficult process. The willingness to enter industrial work, and the conditions under which people did so, belong within the explanation of industrial growth. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "khuzestan-company-towns",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Khuzestan: the reach of the company town",
        "content": "Abadan and Masjed-Soleyman, in Khuzestan Province, became major settings for this transformation. A company town is a settlement in which an employer exercises substantial influence over residential development and everyday infrastructure as well as production. Kaveh Ehsani interprets these oil towns as places where authoritarian planning encountered populations whose diversity and everyday activity exceeded company designs. His account stresses the emergence of dynamic, mixed urban cultures within an unequal institutional setting. [s2]\n\nThe built environment made these inequalities visible. Mattin Biglari's environmental history documents racialized divisions in employment and unequal access to water, electricity, housing, and leisure facilities in early Abadan. Industrial infrastructure and social infrastructure developed together, but their benefits were distributed differently. A refinery's technical sophistication therefore cannot stand as a sufficient measure of surrounding living conditions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "isfahan-manufacturing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Isfahan: from textiles to a diversified industrial region",
        "content": "Isfahan followed a different trajectory, with textile manufacturing becoming an important source of urban employment before the expansion of heavy industry. Encyclopaedia Iranica describes the subsequent development of steel, refining, and other industries alongside transport, water infrastructure, and residential growth. Its historical account connects industrial expansion with the arrival of workers and the incorporation of surrounding settlements into a growing city. [s4][s5]\n\nWorkers also acted collectively over the terms of industrial life. The account of Isfahan's labor movement records organization and strikes in the early 1940s, with demands extending beyond wages to hours, medical provision, food support, and restrictions on child labor. These historical settlements should not be read as statements of present employment law. They show that the social consequences of industrialization were negotiated and contested rather than delivered automatically by investment. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "livelihoods-and-exposure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Livelihoods and environmental burdens",
        "content": "Biglari's study of Abadan examines the relationship between industrial dependence and environmental harm. Drawing on company archives and former workers' and residents' accounts, he argues that pollution was obscured by explanations that attributed ill health to local society rather than refinery operations. His interpretation concerns the refinery's earlier history; it does not measure present-day disease prevalence. [s3]\n\nThe same study identifies a difficult relationship between harm and livelihood: residents depended on the plant even while living with its consequences. Read alongside Isfahan's industrial history, this suggests a useful comparative approach. Examine employment, housing, services, worker organization, and exposure together, while preserving differences between places and periods. Production growth alone cannot explain who gained security or who carried the costs. [s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
